\title{tfhunter: Identification and classification of eukaryotic transcription factors}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

tfhunter is used to identify transcription factors (TFs) from whole-genome protein sequence data of any eukaryotic species, and classify them into families using multiple classification schemes. TFs are identified using pre-built hidden Markov model profiles for DNA-binding domains. Then, auxiliary and forbidden domains are used with DNA-binding domains to classify TFs into families and subfamilies (when applicable). TFs from all eukaryotes can be classified with CIS-BP's scheme, which defines families based on the DNA-binding domains of each TF. Animal TFs can also be classified into the structure-based superclasses, classes, and families of TFClass. Plant TFs can also be classified with the schemes of PlantTFDB (58 families/subfamilies) and TAPscan (over 130 families of transcription-associated proteins), and into the structure-based families, classes, and superclasses of Plant-TFClass. tfhunter is the successor of the Bioconductor package planttfhunter.
}
